  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Strong thunderstorms today through the afternoon and evening. \n   Gusty outflow winds as the main threat. \n\n-  Cold front Sunday brings cooler temperatures but gusty westerly\n   winds and dry air.\n\n-  Ridge rebuilds early next week, warming and drying trend.\n\nPartial clearing across southern and central Idaho has allowed \nfor diurnal heating and weak instability by midday. Showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to increasingly initiate over higher \nterrain early this afternoon over Idaho and Lemhi counties and \ntrack northeastward. Multiple rounds of convection will persist \nthrough the evening, particularly across northwest Montana. The \nprimary hazard will be gusty outflow winds of 35-45 mph, with a \nMarginal Risk (5% probability) for isolated severe gusts exceeding\n58 mph. Frequent lightning (posing a threat for new fire starts) \nand small hail remain secondary concerns.\n\nPrecipitable water (PWAT) values near 1.0 inch, anomalously high \nfor the region will yield a localized threat for excessive runoff \nand flash flooding over steep terrain, urban centers, and burn \nscars. Elevated instability will persist overnight across western \nMontana, maintaining the potential for isolated thunderstorms into\nearly Sunday morning.\n\nA cold front will cross the Northern Rockies late tonight into \nSunday, bringing brief thermal relief as daytime highs drop 10-15 \ndegrees to near seasonal averages. Deep boundary-layer mixing \nbehind the front will combine with a dry post-frontal airmass to \nyield gusty westerly winds. Elevated fire weather conditions are \nexpected across the region Sunday afternoon, potentially critical\nfor parts of southwest Montana.\n\nRidge rebuilding early next week will mark a return to hot and \nmostly dry conditions, though isolated afternoon thunderstorms \nremain possible in Lemhi County and southwestern Montana. By mid-\nto-late next week, ensemble cluster analysis indicates 70% \nconfidence in a return to a trough over the Pacific Northwest.\nThis pattern would support cooling with seasonable daytime \ntemperatures, breezy afternoon winds, and scattered \nshowers/thunderstorms (although not a particularly wet pattern).",
